<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Haiti</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Haiti is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> country that occupies the western part of the island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispaniola">Hispaniola</a>, formerly the French colony of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-domingue">Saint-Domingue</a>. Many of its enslaved population came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a>, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yoruba">Yoruba people</a> from the region of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oyo-empire">Oyo Empire</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haitian-vodou">Haitian Vodou</a> carries elements of Yoruba religion, an example of the Oyo legacy in the Atlantic world.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/haiti.jpg" alt="Haiti" width="320" height="192" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: (colours and size changes of the now deleted versions) Madden, Vzb83, Denelson83, Chanheigeorge, Zscout370 and Nightstallion … · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Haiti.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Haiti forms the western three-eighths of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispaniola">Hispaniola</a>, the second-largest island of the Greater Antilles, and shares a border of 360 km with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominican-republic">Dominican Republic</a>. It is the most mountainous country in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a>, with plains covering only 22% of its territory, and the highest point is Pic la Selle at 2,680 m. The name comes from the Taino language and means land of high mountains. It was the native name for the whole island, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jean-jacques-dessalines">Jean-Jacques Dessalines</a> restored it as the official name of independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-domingue">Saint-Domingue</a>, in tribute to the Amerindian predecessors.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Indigenous peoples have lived on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispaniola">Hispaniola</a> for about 6,000 years. Arawakan-speaking ancestors of the Taino arrived during the 1st millennium BCE, and at the time of European contact the island was divided among five caciquedoms led by chiefs. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus">Christopher Columbus</a> landed on 6 December 1492 and claimed the island for the Crown of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a>. The 39 men he left behind founded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-navidad">La Navidad</a> but were later killed by the Taino.</p><p>European diseases caused epidemics among the native people, the first recorded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smallpox">smallpox</a> epidemic in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a> breaking out on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispaniola">Hispaniola</a> in 1507, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/encomienda">encomienda</a> system, which forced natives to work in gold mines and plantations, reduced their numbers further. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> held the island until 1697, when the western part was ceded to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-domingue">Saint-Domingue</a>, a plantation colony worked by enslaved Africans.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haitian-revolution">Haitian Revolution</a> of 1791 to 1804 made Haiti the first sovereign state in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a> and the first country in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a> to abolish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> officially. The independent state began as an empire, later split into two and reunited in 1820, and a second empire under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/faustin-soulouque">Faustin Soulouque</a> ended with the return of the republic in 1859. The 19th century brought instability, international isolation and a crippling debt to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. United States forces occupied Haiti from 1915 to 1934, and the Duvalier family ruled as dictators from 1957 to 1986.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Haiti is a republic with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/port-au-prince">Port-au-Prince</a> as its capital and largest city, and about 11 million people, the most in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a>. Its culture blends French and African customs with Spanish and Indigenous influences. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/citadelle-laferriere">Citadelle Laferrière</a>, built between 1805 and 1820, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sans-souci-palace">Sans-Souci Palace</a> were inscribed as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a> in 1982.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Haiti” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>By the 1780s Saint-Domingue, now <b>Haiti</b>, produced around 40 per cent of Europe's sugar and some 60 per cent of its coffee.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-empire">French Empire · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Overseas, the kingdom founded New France, with Quebec in 1608, Louisiana and sugar colonies in the Caribbean, leaving French-speaking communities from Canada to <b>Haiti</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-france">Kingdom of France · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Kongo religious and cultural elements persist in Brazil, Cuba, <b>Haiti</b> and elsewhere in the Caribbean.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kongo-empire">Kongo Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The constitution written for <b>Haiti</b> under US occupation in 1915, which for the first time allowed foreigners to own land in the country, was drafted by the future US president Franklin D. Roosevelt.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-haiti">Republic of Haiti · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>The Republic of <b>Haiti</b> arose from the crisis that followed the assassination of Emperor Jacques I, the former Jean-Jacques Dessalines, near Port-au-Prince on 17 October 1806.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-haiti-1806-1849">Republic of Haiti (1806-1849) · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>It culminated in the elimination of slavery and the founding of <b>Haiti</b> over the whole of Hispaniola, and it cost Napoleon so dearly that, having sold Louisiana in April 1803, he lost interest in the Western Hemisphere.</blockquote><a href="/empire/saint-domingue">Saint-Domingue · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
